WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:06.058
[SOUND] Flying robots and

00:00:06.058 --> 00:00:09.377
CIA agents?

00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:14.490
Jack, a man in his twenties diagnosed with
schizophrenia, paced the streets.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.540
He heard buzzing which he attributed to
attacking robots and

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.609
he swung his body around in defense.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.720
He was convinced CIA agents lurked in the
bushes, and so

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.170
he looked around suspiciously.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:30.770
He was completely alone in this world of
imaginary intruders, yet

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.550
he was surrounded by real people.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.560
Jack had not seen a psychiatrist in weeks,
and

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.910
he was off his medications, leaving him
agitated and paranoid.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.980
Like so many times in the past, someone
became fearful and called the police.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.450
Jack was arrested for disturbing the peace
and he was brought to the hospital.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.070
As a medical student,

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.110
I first met Jack in the locked ward where
he was being held for treatment.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.890
As part of the medical team,

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.800
I worked to change his medications to
better control his psychosis.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.890
To make those attacking robots and CIA
agents disappear.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.580
His stay in the hospital was obviously
meant to be therapeutic.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.840
But while he was with us, he missed
several probation hearings for

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:17.609
past defenses.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.800
He lost access to low income housing and
he was kept out of the work force.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.780
Jack's story is all too emblematic

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.090
of those with serious mental illness in
the United States.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:36.825
It is a story characterized by inadequate
solutions, broken systems.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.080
And the need for something more.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.400
Something additional with the capacity to
heal.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.300
Serious mental illness though, is not
limited to Schizophrenia.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.350
It includes any mental illness that causes
significant impairment in daily life,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.610
life activities, like working.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.240
It includes the brother with severe
anxiety, the grandfather with PTSD,

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.070
and the mother with Bipolar Disorder.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.330
Think of those in your life who have
struggled with mental illness.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.190
They are not alone.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.290
There are 44 million American adults with
mental illness.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:18.720
44 million.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.620
That's more than the population of
California.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.210
And there are 10 million American adults
with serious mental illness,

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.920
more than the population of New York City.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.210
These statistics include my own family.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.589
Growing up, I watched loved ones battle
mental illness.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:44.480
I remember sitting in folding chairs at
Alcoholic Anonymous meetings,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.720
listening to poignant stores of relapse
and recovery.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.730
I remember patiently waiting for

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.030
the visiting hours to begin at the
residential center for substance abuse.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.060
As much as I wanted to help, I was a kid.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.675
Granted I was a scrappy kid.

00:03:00.675 --> 00:03:02.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I was still a kid who had enough

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.955
trouble talking to girls or choosing a
user name for AOL Instant Messenger.

00:03:06.955 --> 00:03:09.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A massive, massive challenge.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:14.040
And so clearly I was limited in what I
could do.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:18.210
These experiences.

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:22.670
This feeling of helplessness, sparked my
initial interest in mental illness,

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.330
and medicine.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.550
I became convinced that if I wanted to
help those with serious mental illness

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:34.060
like Jack, if I wanted to be a resource to
my family, I needed to become a doctor.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.348
Full disclosure, I also thought the
doctors on ER were bad ass.

00:03:39.348 --> 00:03:44.250
[LAUGH] So I had mixed motives, but I
convinced Stanford to take me in.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.480
And I immediately started looking for
opportunities to get involved.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.450
I joined a research team that studies
early childhood trauma and

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.940
designed psychiatric services for low
income communities.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.309
I managed a mental health clinic for
adults without insurance.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.960
These experiences deepened my resolve to
address mental illness, but

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.200
they also gave me insight into the very
real constraints of my chosen field.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.700
It's true I met doctors who treated
patients with compassion and skill.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.270
But just a fraction of those who needed
treatment got it.

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:23.480
And often after waiting a long time and as
with other diseases,

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.920
it's really tough to monitor, because
patients are rarely in the clinic.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:30.740
I also got exposure to the role of
government.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:36.630
I met a bunch of patients who went months,
if not years with no treatment,

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.610
who finally got connected to doctors,
thanks to government programs and clinics.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:46.840
The reality though, is these efforts are
totally insufficient.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.310
I gradually came to this realization,

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.600
but it was my experience with Jack that
really crystalized it.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.250
I recognized that Jack needed more than
what healthcare and

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.200
government could offer.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.430
To lead a healthier, fuller life, Jack
needed an additional ally.

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:11.750
So what do you do in the case of serious
mental illness when health care and

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:12.740
government fall short?

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.395
I decided to apply to business school.

00:05:17.395 --> 00:05:18.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was either this or

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.235
management consulting.

00:05:19.235 --> 00:05:21.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think I made the right decision.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:26.910
But after spending many years in the Bay
area, I really grew an appreciation for

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:31.070
the ability of business to create products
and solutions for unmet needs.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.340
And I saw tremendous potential for its
application to serious mental illness.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.230
I came to the conclusion, that as business
leaders,

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:42.230
we are uniquely positioned to tackle the
problems made visible by Jack's story.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.970
Every day we see, all around us, how
business can grow economies,

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:52.070
build personal fortune, and transform the
world.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.570
I argue business also has the capacity to
heal.

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:04.000
Since starting business school, I've come
across a number of companies in

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.089
the Bay Area that are working on mental
illness.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:12.530
I'm here today not talking to you as an
entrepreneur, an investor or

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:16.630
an employee but rather as a family member
and as a future doctor.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:21.860
I want to share just two companies that
have developed products that are enabling

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.549
physicians to better treat those with
serious mental illness.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:29.990
These products are not yet widely
available, but they offer a glimpse

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.980
into the potential of business, it's
potential, and its capacity to heal.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.060
Jack's illness is not static.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.980
We met him in the hospital, but he can go
long stretches of time, in the community,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.880
doing just fine.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:50.150
But when he has a psychotic break, his
schizophrenia is totally debilitating.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:55.165
Each episode sets him further and further
back, and intervening early is tough.

00:06:55.165 --> 00:06:58.920
Ginger IO might offer a solution.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:04.420
It's a mobile app that collects data on a
patient's mobility, communication, and

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:05.040
sleep patterns.

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:09.210
With this information, as Jack's doctor

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.737
I could know when his behavior is making a
major departure from his normal routine.

00:07:13.737 --> 00:07:18.700
Ginger IO would enable me to anticipate

00:07:18.700 --> 00:07:22.700
Jack's psychotic breaks and to intervene
before hospitalization.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:29.910
I'd be the first one to tell you that
Jack's medications are not perfect, but

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:32.830
he functions and he feels a lot better
when he takes them.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:37.330
But he's just like the rest of us and he
often forgets to take his medications.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.150
Proteus Digital Health,

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:43.160
the second company is working to improve
medication adherence.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:49.230
It has developed a tiny, ingestible sensor
that fits inside individual pills.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:52.910
When you take the pill, it's activated by
the fluid in your stomach and

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.520
a signal is sent to a patch on your skin.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:58.330
That patch in turn communicates with a
smart phone,

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.110
which collects data on medication
adherence.

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.250
That's pretty cool, right?

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:05.515
Yeah, it is.

00:08:05.515 --> 00:08:08.820
[LAUGH]
Stay with me.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.500
With Proteus as Jack's doctor, I could
know when he is off his medications,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.920
and I could help him reach his goal of
leading a healthier life.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:25.169
Imagine what future solutions could be
designed if more business leaders like us.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.630
Applied our creativity and our acumen
toward serious mental illness.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:36.300
Imagine how Jack's life could be different
if he had access to Ginger IO and Proteus

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:40.800
and now take that impact and multiply it
by the ten million other American adults

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.890
with serious mental illness and this way
business has a capacity to heal.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:52.490
Our inadequate response to serious mental
illness is a glaring example

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:56.360
of our collective failure to serve some of
the weakest members in our society.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.940
While this failure is largely hidden,
mental illness touches all of our lives.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.360
Business and technology are not perfect
solutions.

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:08.980
But they have incredible potential to
help.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:11.960
As business leaders,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:15.400
we have the ability to transform what is
today to what can be tomorrow.

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:20.330
My journey, which now includes medicine
and business, is still uncharted,

00:09:20.330 --> 00:09:21.090
as is yours.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.150
As you leave today,

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:27.950
and as you look to your own future, I
simply ask you to be more inclusive.

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:32.094
To include Jack as you go out to reshape
the world.

00:09:32.094 --> 00:09:32.837
Thank you.

00:09:32.837 --> 00:09:35.857
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:35.857 --> 00:09:40.809
[MUSIC]

